-- Passage rg6-context-clues-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a98ba625-18e8-5214-9b7f-88deae86109f', 'rg6-context-clues-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Rivers Above the Ground$q$, $q$More than two thousand years ago, the city of Rome had a problem. Its population was growing quickly, and the wells and nearby river could not supply enough clean water for everyone. Roman engineers solved this problem by building aqueducts, which were long channels that carried water into the city from springs in the hills.

An aqueduct worked without any pumps or motors. Instead, it relied on gravity. Engineers designed each channel with a slight incline, a downward slope so gentle that the water dropped only a few feet over many miles. If the slope was too steep, the rushing water would wear away the channel. If it was too flat, the water would stop flowing.

Most of an aqueduct actually ran underground, hidden inside tunnels. Where the channel had to cross a valley, however, workers built tall bridges with rows of arches. These bridges are the parts most people picture today.

The Romans built their aqueducts to be durable. They used stone, brick, and a strong type of concrete that hardened even underwater. Some sections still stand after two thousand years, and a few still carry water.

Thanks to the aqueducts, water in Rome became abundant. There was plenty for public fountains, public baths, and gardens. Ordinary people could fill jugs at a fountain near their homes instead of walking to the river.

Keeping the system working took constant effort. Crews of workers had to maintain the channels by clearing out mud, patching cracks, and removing the hard mineral crust that built up on the walls. Without this care, even the best aqueduct would eventually clog and fail.$q$, 'draft')
