-- Passage rg6-context-clues-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7504da2c-f357-58de-871a-dccd9a225ef7', 'rg6-context-clues-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Mud on the Course$q$, $q$The morning of the county cross-country meet, rain had fallen all night. By the time Kenji Watanabe reached the starting line, the course was a treacherous mess. Runners in the earlier race had slipped on the hills, and one boy had lost a shoe in a puddle of thick mud.

Coach Alvarez gathered the team under a tent. "Do not sprint at the start," she told them. "Find a steady pace and hold it. A speed you can keep for the whole race will beat a fast start that leaves you exhausted."

Kenji nodded, but when the starting horn sounded, he forgot everything. He shot to the front with the leaders. For the first half mile, he felt amazing.

Then came the long hill. His legs felt heavy, and his breath came in ragged gasps. Kenji realized he did not have the stamina to keep up with the leaders for two more miles. Runners he had passed earlier began to pass him back, one after another.

Kenji wanted to stop. Instead, he remembered his coach's words. He slowed down, kept his arms relaxed, and counted his steps to calm himself. He regained his composure, and his breathing grew smoother. On the final stretch, he even caught two runners who had started too fast, just as he had.

He crossed the finish line in twenty-first place, his shoes caked with mud. It was not his best result, but it was not a disaster either.

"You recovered well," Coach Alvarez said, handing him a water bottle. "Next time, run the first mile with your head instead of your legs."$q$, 'draft')
