-- Passage rg6-context-clues-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('867e0745-bfe3-55a8-8ef4-be94739c2cfe', 'rg6-context-clues-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Printing in Three Dimensions$q$, $q$A regular printer puts ink on flat paper. A 3D printer does something that seems almost like magic: it builds solid objects you can hold. Schools, hospitals, and factories now use these machines for many jobs.

Most 3D printers work by melting a thin plastic thread called filament. The printer pushes the hot filament through a tiny nozzle, which moves back and forth to draw one flat shape. Then it rises slightly and draws another shape on top. Layer by layer, the object grows taller, a bit like a stack of very thin pancakes. A small toy might need two hundred layers.

One big advantage of 3D printing is speed in testing ideas. Before a company makes thousands of a new product, designers often build a prototype, an early model used to see whether the design works. In the past, making a single prototype could take weeks. With a 3D printer, it might take one afternoon.

The machines are also remarkably versatile. The same printer can make a replacement knob for a stove, a model of a dinosaur skull, or a custom grip for a person who has trouble holding a pencil. Doctors have even used printed models of hearts to plan operations.

Printers can create shapes that would be nearly impossible to carve by hand. A printed object can be intricate, full of tiny tunnels, twists, and patterns packed into a small space.

Still, 3D printing has limits. Large objects take many hours, and plastic parts are not as strong as metal ones. For now, factories still use older methods when they need to make millions of identical items quickly and cheaply.$q$, 'draft')
