insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6487fd3f-6c47-50ed-91c0-c2f89aff8975', 'rg6-context-clues-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Memory Quilt$q$, $q$Zara Adebayo had never sewn anything more complicated than a button. So when her grandmother suggested they make a quilt together over winter break, Zara was not sure what to expect.

Nana opened a cardboard box full of old clothes. There was a faded flannel shirt that had belonged to Zara's grandfather, a yellow dress Zara had worn at age four, and a pair of jeans with frayed knees, their threads hanging loose like fringe.

"We will salvage the good parts," Nana said. "Nothing in this box is ready for the trash. We just have to rescue the pieces worth keeping."

First they cut the fabric into squares. Nana was meticulous about it. She measured every square twice, lined up her ruler with care, and made Zara redo any square that was even a little crooked.

Then came the pinning. Zara had to pin each square to its neighbor, row after row, hundreds of pins in all. By the second afternoon, she found the work tedious. She sighed loudly and asked if they could just glue everything instead.

Nana laughed. "The boring parts are what hold it together," she said.

By the end of break, the quilt was finished. It was not perfect. One corner puckered, and a blue square sat slightly sideways. But when Zara spread it across her bed, she could see her whole family in it.

"Someday this will be an heirloom," Nana said. "You will hand it to your own children, the way my mother handed me her sewing box."

Zara ran her hand over the flannel square and decided she would take very good care of it.$q$, 'draft')
