-- Passage rg6-context-clues-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('25a2c19d-abb7-5486-a0ac-7d3e712c08fb', 'rg6-context-clues-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Frog That Freezes$q$, $q$In the forests of Alaska and Canada, winter temperatures can stay far below freezing for months. Many animals escape the cold by flying south. The wood frog, a small brown frog about two inches long, does something much stranger. It freezes.

As autumn ends, the wood frog crawls under a layer of dead leaves on the forest floor. When the temperature falls, ice begins to form in its body. Its heart stops beating, and it stops breathing. For weeks at a time, the frog stays dormant, showing no signs of life at all.

Ordinarily, freezing would be deadly for an animal. Ice crystals have sharp edges, and they can tear the tiny cells that make up living things. The wood frog survives because of a clever defense. As it starts to freeze, its liver floods its body with sugar. The sugar acts like a shield inside each cell, keeping ice from forming there. Ice fills the spaces between cells instead, where it does less harm.

When spring arrives, the frog thaws. The ice melts, starting from the inside, and within a few hours the heart begins to beat again. Soon the frog hops away to find a pond.

Scientists admire how resilient the wood frog is. It can freeze and thaw several times in a single winter and still recover each time.

Researchers hope that learning from the wood frog could someday help doctors. Organs donated for transplants spoil quickly once they are removed from the body. If scientists could find a way to freeze organs safely, the frog's secret might help many patients.$q$, 'draft')
