-- Passage rg6-context-clues-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0f744b93-f440-5960-b80f-0c7dc42bfd70', 'rg6-context-clues-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Orange Kite$q$, $q$Every spring, the town of Riverbend held a kite festival in the park beside the river. This year, Ana Lucia Torres and her neighbor Dev Kapoor decided to build their own kite instead of buying one.

Neither of them had ever built a kite before. They were complete novices, so they borrowed a book from the library and followed the instructions step by step. They made the frame from two thin wooden rods tied in a cross. Then they stretched a sheet of bright orange plastic over it and added a long tail made of ribbons.

Dev's older sister, Meera, was skeptical. "That thing will never get off the ground," she said, folding her arms. "Homemade kites usually just crash."

On the day of the festival, the sky was clear, but the wind was erratic. One moment the air was perfectly still. The next, a strong gust would whip across the field, then disappear just as quickly.

Ana Lucia held the string while Dev ran with the kite. On the first try, it spun in circles and smacked into the grass. On the second try, the tail tangled around a rod. They untangled it, made the tail longer to keep the kite steady, and tried a third time.

This time, a gust caught the orange sail, and the kite soared. It climbed higher and higher until it was a tiny bright dot above the trees. People on the hill pointed and cheered.

At the end of the day, the judges gave Ana Lucia and Dev a ribbon for the best homemade kite. Even Meera admitted she had been wrong. "Next year," she said, "can I help you build one?"$q$, 'draft')
